ible by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>33</mn></math>&nbsp;are</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\"><mrow><mn>1023</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1056</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2772</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Let total numbers be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>, then</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2772</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1023</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>33</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>54</mn></math></p>Number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>-digit numbers that are less than or equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2800</mn></math> and either divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> or by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math> , is equal to<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>164</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>54</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>710</mn></math></p>",
